    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** The decision of which address form to use, normalization of each field, and composition of the flat `address` all live in `OwnerAddress.apply(OwnerFieldsDto)`. That's the class whose documented purpose is \"canonical address normalization for the owner pipelines,\" so this is its natural home. The new method builds on the existing `normalize` primitive \u2014 a reasonable two-level layering (primitive + orchestrator), not an inconsistent grab-bag.\n\n- **No duplication.** Both `ValidateNewOwner` (create) and `ValidateOwner` (update) delegate to the single `OwnerAddress.apply`, replacing what would otherwise be duplicated form-selection/composition logic. The \"normalize address up front\" pattern that already existed in `ValidateNewOwner` is preserved and extended symmetrically to the update path.\n\n- **Readers stay untouched \u2014 the right call.** By composing the structured fields into the flat `address` at the source, everything downstream that reads `address` (household hash, postcode validation, locality in `BuildOwner`/`Household`) transparently gets the structured form with no changes. This satisfies the \"use structured when present, fall back to flat\" requirement via composition-at-source rather than scattering branching across every reader \u2014 the coherent, additive choice.\n\n- **Create/update parity is maintained in each path's own idiom.** `ApplyOwner` (update) explicitly copies the two new fields; `BuildOwner` (create) continues to rely on `OwnerMapper.toOwner`, which maps the matching DTO fields automatically. Each addition follows the mechanism already established in its pipeline.\n\n- Model, the four schema dialects, OpenAPI, and the required-field contract are all extended consistently, and `OwnerAddress.apply` mutating the DTO in place matches the project's documented `Apply<E>` \"mutate in place\" idiom.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this natural; no restructuring needed.",
